.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-SET-BUCKET-VERSIONING 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 2.1.3" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-set-bucket-versioning \- keep replaced objects as versions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm set-bucket-versioning
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME|ERN
.BR \-\-enabled | \-e
.IR true | false
.SH DESCRIPTION
Turns versioning on or off for a bucket.
.PP
With versioning on, an upload to a key that already holds an object is
compared with that object by MD5, once all of its bytes have arrived:
.TP
.B Different bytes
The upload becomes the current object, with a new
.IR versionId .
The object it replaces is kept as a non-current version: its file stays
on disk and its row moves to the version store. An
.I esm.object.updated
event is published, as for any upload over an existing key.
.TP
.B The same bytes
The upload is ignored. Nothing is stored, the current object is left as
it was, and no event is published. The answer describes the object that
was already there and carries
.BR "\(dqunchanged\(dq: true" .
For a multipart upload, the bytes are compared after
.I complete-upload
has assembled them, and the object is
.I COMPLETED
again once that is done.
.PP
A first upload to a key, and every upload to a bucket without versioning,
behaves as it always has: the object is stored, and in a bucket without
versioning an upload over an existing key replaces it, file and all, even
when the bytes are the same.
.PP
Turning versioning off makes uploads replace objects again. The versions
already kept stay until they are deleted.
.SH WORKING WITH VERSIONS
.TP
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-object-versions (1)
lists a key's versions, the current one first.
.TP
.BR euclid-cli-esm-download-file (1) " \-\-version\-id"
downloads an earlier version.
.TP
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-object (1) " \-\-version\-id"
deletes one version. Without it, deleting an object deletes every version
of it.
.PP
Moving or renaming an object takes its versions with it. Copying it does
not: the copy starts a history of its own. A move into a bucket whose
encryption differs is refused while the object has non-current versions,
since their files would all have to be re-encrypted.
.PP
Purging or deleting a bucket removes the versions of every object it
removes.
.SH WHAT VERSIONS COST
Each non-current version keeps its file. The bucket's
.I size
and
.I objects
counters describe its current objects only, so they do not show the disk
the versions use;
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-object-versions (1)
gives each version's size. Lifecycle rules archive current objects only.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME|ERN\fR
The bucket, by name or ERN. Required.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-enabled " " \fItrue\fR|\fIfalse\fR
Whether versioning is on. Required: either change may not be what
somebody meant, and the two cost very different amounts of disk.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The setting was changed. The bucket's ERN, name and versioning flag are
printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, no such
bucket exists, or the caller may not change it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm set-bucket-versioning --bucket incoming --enabled true

euclid-cli esm upload-file --bucket incoming --key feed.xml --file feed.xml
euclid-cli esm upload-file --bucket incoming --key feed.xml --file feed.xml   # "unchanged": true
euclid-cli esm upload-file --bucket incoming --key feed.xml --file feed-v2.xml
euclid-cli esm list-object-versions --bucket incoming --key feed.xml
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-object-versions (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-download-file (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-object (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket (1)
